‪-Sure.
‪-Okay.

686
00:28:24,911 --> 00:28:26,204
‪-Ms. Yi Do.
‪-Yes?

687
00:28:26,996 --> 00:28:30,166
‪I believe we're supposed to
‪start over here first.

688
00:28:30,250 --> 00:28:33,837
‪It feels like they're basically telling us
‪to start here because it's the easiest.

689
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:36,798
‪You'll see a bird, a person, a cow,

690
00:28:36,881 --> 00:28:38,633
‪a spider, and a snake here.

691
00:28:38,716 --> 00:28:39,717
‪Yeah, I see them all.

692
00:28:39,801 --> 00:28:43,012
‪A few characters are straightforward
‪and resemble the objects they represent.

693
00:28:44,305 --> 00:28:46,057
‪What's that Citroën logo?

694
00:28:46,141 --> 00:28:47,517
‪What's a Citroën logo?

695
00:28:47,600 --> 00:28:48,852
‪These two chevrons.

696
00:28:48,935 --> 00:28:51,855
‪Our guess was "to face upward."

697
00:28:52,355 --> 00:28:54,274
‪"The birds face upward"
‪and "The human face upward."

698
00:28:54,357 --> 00:28:55,942
‪They're all looking up.

699
00:28:56,901 --> 00:29:00,447
‪This was the first thing we confirmed.

700
00:29:00,530 --> 00:29:02,073
‪-Water.
‪-This is written on the bread hatch.

701
00:29:02,157 --> 00:29:03,950
‪We think it means
‪"something to be consumed."

702
00:29:04,492 --> 00:29:07,996
‪So the dot on top marks verbs.

703
00:29:08,747 --> 00:29:11,040
‪-Then this must be "to consume."
‪-Correct.

704
00:29:11,124 --> 00:29:11,958
‪-Right.
‪-Exactly.

705
00:29:12,041 --> 00:29:14,669
‪So these sentences are about the cow
‪and the others eating something.

706
00:29:14,753 --> 00:29:16,004
‪Exactly. I told you she'd get it.

707
00:29:16,796 --> 00:29:18,214
‪What about this section here?

708
00:29:19,424 --> 00:29:20,967
‪That's the hardest part. The core of it.

709
00:29:21,801 --> 00:29:23,803
‪This is "the eye in the sky."

710
00:29:23,887 --> 00:29:25,764
‪It's referring to the sun.

711
00:29:25,847 --> 00:29:28,308
‪-"The eye in the sky."
‪-Because this eye is up in the sky.

712
00:29:29,726 --> 00:29:33,188
‪The only confirmed character
‪we have here is "to observe."

713
00:29:33,813 --> 00:29:35,565
‪And this is "the sun."

714
00:29:36,733 --> 00:29:39,903
‪But our knowledge of grammar
‪for these hieroglyphics

715
00:29:39,986 --> 00:29:41,571
‪-is still pretty limited.
‪-Right.

716
00:29:41,654 --> 00:29:43,948
‪-It might be faster to work on these.
‪-Yeah.

717
00:29:44,032 --> 00:29:45,742
‪-Just focus on the sentences?
‪-On forming sentences.

718
00:29:45,825 --> 00:29:47,410
‪Yeah, I think that too.

719
00:29:47,494 --> 00:29:50,747
‪-It could point us in the right direction.
‪-Why don't all four of us dive in

720
00:29:50,830 --> 00:29:52,165
‪and form as many sentences as we can?

721
00:29:52,248 --> 00:29:54,459
‪We need to connect these into sentences.

722
00:29:55,084 --> 00:29:57,420
‪First, we need to find
‪the connecting patterns.

723
00:29:57,504 --> 00:29:59,714
‪Some are vertical.

724
00:30:00,340 --> 00:30:01,841
‪Vertical or horizontal.

725
00:30:02,467 --> 00:30:04,260
‪And there are
‪several single characters too.

726
00:30:05,094 --> 00:30:07,555
‪Some are sentence-ending markers
‪with a full stop.

727
00:30:08,681 --> 00:30:10,266
‪-"You shall do this."
‪-"You should do that."

728
00:30:10,350 --> 00:30:11,559
‪"You should do it. You must do it."

729
00:30:11,643 --> 00:30:12,477
‪There's another one.

730
00:30:13,770 --> 00:30:15,730
‪Should we separate
‪the sentence-ending markers?

731
00:30:15,814 --> 00:30:17,816
‪-Good idea.
‪-Let's separate them.

732
00:30:19,859 --> 00:30:22,403
‪We won't need all these slabs, right?

733
00:30:22,487 --> 00:30:23,696
‪Most are single characters.

734
00:30:23,780 --> 00:30:26,574
‪-I think they'll all be used.
‪-You think so?

735
00:30:26,658 --> 00:30:28,493
‪All of these are gonna be used?

736
00:30:28,576 --> 00:30:32,580
‪-When flipped?
‪-I'm assuming there are no decoys.

737
00:30:32,664 --> 00:30:34,874
‪But that's 7-by-7, so 49 total.

738
00:30:34,958 --> 00:30:36,292
‪Wouldn't there have to be decoys?

739
00:30:36,376 --> 00:30:39,420
‪That's a 7-by-7 grid.

740
00:30:39,504 --> 00:30:40,713
‪Won't there be many decoys?

741
00:30:40,797 --> 00:30:44,634
‪I don't think we're supposed to
‪move them over there.

742
00:30:44,717 --> 00:30:48,096
‪-I think this content is one set of rules.
‪-That's possible.

743
00:30:48,179 --> 00:30:50,431
‪There was a part in the notice
‪at the entrance

744
00:30:50,515 --> 00:30:54,477
‪that said we'd need
‪tremendous perseverance.

745
00:30:54,561 --> 00:30:56,813
‪-I think the goal is to solve this.
‪-And test our patience?

746
00:30:56,896 --> 00:30:58,314
‪That's what I think.

747
00:30:58,398 --> 00:31:01,317
‪Among the two-character ones…

748
00:31:01,401 --> 00:31:02,235
‪Some make sense, right?

749
00:31:02,318 --> 00:31:05,989
‪Right, look for the ones that make sense
‪or need something to come before them.

750
00:31:06,072 --> 00:31:07,824
‪Look, like this ordinal marker.

751
00:31:08,449 --> 00:31:11,160
‪You think we'd get instructions like
‪"First, do this. Second, do that"?

752
00:31:11,244 --> 00:31:12,996
‪The ordinal marker can only be combined

753
00:31:13,079 --> 00:31:15,206
‪with "one" or "two"
‪to make "first" or "second."

754
00:31:15,290 --> 00:31:20,879
‪Then we have characters like this
‪that are hard to fit anywhere.

755
00:31:20,962 --> 00:31:27,427
‪There were these single characters
‪that were clearly part of a noun.

756
00:31:27,510 --> 00:31:29,596
<i>‪For example, there was </i>‪"heom."

757
00:31:29,679 --> 00:31:32,557
‪Since this is a space
‪where we're being tested,

758
00:31:32,640 --> 00:31:36,477
<i>‪we thought it could be part of </i>‪siheom,
<i>‪as in "test," so we linked that up.</i>

759
00:31:36,561 --> 00:31:38,313
<i>‪There was also </i>‪"beop,"

760
00:31:38,396 --> 00:31:40,899
<i>‪which could be part of </i>‪bangbeop,
<i>‪as in "way."</i>

761
00:31:40,982 --> 00:31:43,192
<i>‪We started combining them</i>
<i>‪one by one in that manner</i>

762
00:31:43,276 --> 00:31:45,778
<i>‪and managed to form a few sets.</i>

763
00:31:45,862 --> 00:31:47,739
‪Then this must be "pass."

764
00:31:48,531 --> 00:31:49,866
‪"Pass."

765
00:31:52,410 --> 00:31:53,494
‪"If you

766
00:31:54,120 --> 00:31:55,955
‪can pass through this place…"

767
00:31:56,039 --> 00:31:57,248
‪-That makes sense.
‪-Yeah.

768
00:31:57,999 --> 00:32:00,585
‪"Pass through this place."

769
00:32:01,169 --> 00:32:03,796
‪I guess "life" and "death"
‪can go together.

770
00:32:04,380 --> 00:32:06,799
‪"Life and death."

771
00:32:07,508 --> 00:32:09,677
‪That one could be <i>‪salgyeol,</i>
‪as in "skin texture."

772
00:32:12,388 --> 00:32:13,473
‪That has to be it, right?

773
00:32:13,556 --> 00:32:15,975
‪I mean, what else can you make with <i>‪gyeol</i>‪?

774
00:32:16,059 --> 00:32:17,518
‪"Skin texture"?

775
00:32:17,602 --> 00:32:18,561
‪With <i>‪gyeol</i>‪,

776
00:32:18,645 --> 00:32:20,855
‪the only pairing I can think of
‪is with <i>‪sal.</i>

777
00:32:20,939 --> 00:32:22,690
‪SALGYEOL, SKIN TEXTURE

778
00:32:23,441 --> 00:32:25,360
‪Wait, maybe not.

779
00:32:25,944 --> 00:32:27,528
<i>‪Sal</i>‪ can mean "survive,"
‪so "You will be able to survive."

780
00:32:28,988 --> 00:32:30,406
‪I think that makes more sense.

781
00:32:30,490 --> 00:32:31,491
‪Then what about <i>‪gyeol?</i>

782
00:32:31,574 --> 00:32:32,867
‪Maybe <i>‪sumgyeol,</i>‪ as in "breath"?

783
00:32:33,493 --> 00:32:34,494
‪Breath…

784
00:32:36,371 --> 00:32:37,413
<i>‪Daegyeol</i>‪, as in "match."

785
00:32:38,414 --> 00:32:39,374
‪No way.

786
00:32:40,667 --> 00:32:42,585
‪So you have to win in a match.

787
00:32:43,336 --> 00:32:45,505
‪Of course. It can't be "skin texture."

788
00:32:45,588 --> 00:32:46,881
‪That makes no sense.

789
00:32:46,965 --> 00:32:48,424
‪I thought it was a love letter.

790
00:32:48,508 --> 00:32:49,926
‪"Soft skin texture."

791
00:32:51,552 --> 00:32:53,137
‪You can sleep if you want.

792
00:32:53,221 --> 00:32:56,015
‪-Come on now.
‪-I'm just choosing to do this.

793
00:33:07,068 --> 00:33:08,820
‪Stella, you did enough.

794
00:33:09,779 --> 00:33:12,323
‪-I think we do need some sleep.
‪-Yeah, we do.

795
00:33:14,534 --> 00:33:15,451
‪Have a good night.

796
00:33:15,535 --> 00:33:16,911
‪Good night, everyone.

797
00:33:36,848 --> 00:33:39,726
<i>‪Yesterday, we didn't even find out</i>
<i>‪what we were supposed to do.</i>

798
00:33:39,809 --> 00:33:42,353
‪So I couldn't just sleep.

799
00:33:42,437 --> 00:33:44,564
‪Sleep can wait until I get home,

800
00:33:44,647 --> 00:33:48,943
<i>‪so I decided to stay up</i>
<i>‪and work on it through the night.</i>

801
00:33:53,406 --> 00:33:54,282
‪Time…

802
00:34:02,373 --> 00:34:03,624
‪"Win the match…"

803
00:34:04,500 --> 00:34:08,379
‪"Only two can…"

804
00:34:09,297 --> 00:34:11,591
‪"survive."

805
00:34:11,674 --> 00:34:18,639
‪ONLY THE TWO WHO WIN THE MATCH CAN SURVIVE

806
00:34:27,440 --> 00:34:28,274
‪"There…

807
00:34:29,692 --> 00:34:30,985
‪will be life."

808
00:34:31,069 --> 00:34:32,320
‪"Death."

809
00:34:32,403 --> 00:34:33,946
‪"Life and death…"

810
00:34:34,030 --> 00:34:34,864
‪"Exist."

811
00:34:34,947 --> 00:34:36,991
‪"Neither life nor death."
‪That's also a possibility.

812
00:34:37,075 --> 00:34:38,326
‪We have "neither."

813
00:34:39,827 --> 00:34:41,788
‪"This place is neither life nor death"?

814
00:34:41,871 --> 00:34:44,373
‪-Doesn't that sound a bit awkward?
‪-Yeah.

815
00:34:44,457 --> 00:34:46,334
‪"Life…"

816
00:34:47,085 --> 00:34:47,919
‪"Life…"

817
00:34:49,212 --> 00:34:50,505
‪It's morning.

818
00:34:50,588 --> 00:34:51,881
‪He's awake.

819
00:34:53,007 --> 00:34:54,717
‪-What time did you get up?
‪-Good morning.

820
00:34:54,801 --> 00:34:57,178
‪We woke up about 30 minutes
‪to an hour ago.

821
00:34:57,720 --> 00:34:59,764
‪At least it's nice and bright
‪in the morning.

822
00:35:04,727 --> 00:35:06,187
‪From what I've gathered,

823
00:35:06,270 --> 00:35:09,899
‪this is set up so two out of four survive,
‪then one out of two.

824
00:35:10,608 --> 00:35:13,861
‪So I'm not sharing any more information.

825
00:35:15,196 --> 00:35:18,491
‪I'll be playing solo
‪after this Korean puzzle.

826
00:35:19,534 --> 00:35:21,786
‪I shared way too much yesterday.

827
00:35:24,664 --> 00:35:28,292
‪This part is a team game,
‪but everything after isn't.

828
00:35:29,585 --> 00:35:30,878
‪Are you sure about that?

829
00:35:32,755 --> 00:35:34,465
‪Shouldn't we play it safe?

830
00:35:34,966 --> 00:35:36,134
‪I don't think so.

831
00:35:36,217 --> 00:35:39,137
‪I doubt any of us can solve this
‪if we don't work together as a team.

832
00:35:40,096 --> 00:35:44,183
‪But based on the words we have now,

833
00:35:44,809 --> 00:35:46,185
‪not everyone can survive.

834
00:35:46,686 --> 00:35:48,354
‪I wanna win.

835
00:35:48,437 --> 00:35:50,857
‪I wanna go back to <i>‪The Devil's Plan.</i>

836
00:35:51,649 --> 00:35:54,360
<i>‪Looking at the Korean puzzle,</i>

837
00:35:54,443 --> 00:35:58,114
<i>‪I could make out sentences</i>
<i>‪like "X out of Y survive."</i>

838
00:35:58,197 --> 00:36:01,450
‪That meant
‪we'll inevitably end up as competitors.

839
00:36:03,452 --> 00:36:05,830
<i>‪She just sapped all my energy</i>
<i>‪the second I woke up.</i>

840
00:36:05,913 --> 00:36:08,624
<i>‪We should be helping each other,</i>
<i>‪no matter what happens.</i>

841
00:36:09,500 --> 00:36:10,877
‪She's an interesting one.

842
00:36:11,460 --> 00:36:13,337
‪I thought sleeping on it

843
00:36:14,213 --> 00:36:16,465
‪would clear things up a bit,
‪but I was wrong.

844
00:36:16,549 --> 00:36:19,302
‪My mind is completely fogged over.

845
00:36:21,596 --> 00:36:22,430
‪What could it be?

846
00:36:26,726 --> 00:36:28,269
‪Hey, something's happening.

847
00:36:28,352 --> 00:36:29,270
‪Now?

848
00:36:29,353 --> 00:36:30,897
‪-Are we doing something now?
‪-Yeah.

849
00:36:36,736 --> 00:36:38,905
‪Maybe something happens in the morning
‪at a certain time.

850
00:36:41,240 --> 00:36:43,534
‪-Is it just an alarm?
‪-How many times did it go off?

851
00:36:45,286 --> 00:36:46,787
‪Out of nowhere,

852
00:36:47,496 --> 00:36:50,082
‪there was this really loud sound,
‪almost like a bird call.

853
00:36:50,166 --> 00:36:51,584
‪Everyone was caught off guard,

854
00:36:51,667 --> 00:36:52,877
‪especially Jin-ho.

855
00:36:52,960 --> 00:36:54,212
‪What was that?

856
00:36:54,295 --> 00:36:55,379
‪An alarm?

857
00:36:55,463 --> 00:36:56,380
‪No idea.

858
00:36:58,799 --> 00:37:02,303
‪Us two dads are looking rough.

859
00:37:05,765 --> 00:37:08,267
‪Why did the cuckoo suddenly go off?

860
00:37:08,351 --> 00:37:10,561
‪Why did that crazy bird
‪go off all of a sudden?

861
00:37:12,313 --> 00:37:14,899
‪The bird… We still don't know
‪what this is, right?

862
00:37:16,317 --> 00:37:20,446
‪Maybe it's something like,
‪"They ate a person who was attacked."

863
00:37:22,823 --> 00:37:24,367
‪The attacked person…

864
00:37:24,450 --> 00:37:28,329
‪ATTACKED, ATTACK

865
00:37:28,412 --> 00:37:31,624
‪That "A" might not be "to attack."

866
00:37:32,416 --> 00:37:34,252
‪It might be "to kill."

867
00:37:34,335 --> 00:37:35,544
‪-Kill?
‪-Yeah.

868
00:37:36,837 --> 00:37:38,130
‪It's "to die."

869
00:37:38,798 --> 00:37:41,717
‪The person consumed poison and died.

870
00:37:42,635 --> 00:37:45,846
‪So, "When a person consumes poison,
‪the person dies."

871
00:37:46,347 --> 00:37:49,433
‪They're dead in both of these.

872
00:37:49,517 --> 00:37:53,521
‪"Attacked person" doesn't make sense,
‪but "dead person" does.

873
00:37:54,814 --> 00:37:58,150
‪Did we figure out what these chevrons are?

874
00:37:58,734 --> 00:38:03,489
‪We're not sure,
‪but we instinctively thought "upward."

875
00:38:03,572 --> 00:38:05,449
‪This is "downward," right?

876
00:38:05,533 --> 00:38:07,201
‪No, this is "to face."

877
00:38:07,285 --> 00:38:10,871
‪-I think so.
‪-It's still just speculation at best.

878
00:38:10,955 --> 00:38:12,248
‪TO FACE?

879
00:38:12,331 --> 00:38:15,376
‪Birds make sounds, and so do people.

880
00:38:18,254 --> 00:38:19,630
‪Could it be "to make sound"?

881
00:38:20,172 --> 00:38:22,842
‪-Oh, that one?
‪-Look.

882
00:38:23,718 --> 00:38:25,136
‪Birds can chirp.

883
00:38:25,803 --> 00:38:27,263
‪People can talk.

884
00:38:28,014 --> 00:38:30,558
‪But fruit can't speak.

885
00:38:30,641 --> 00:38:31,642
‪That makes sense.

886
00:38:32,727 --> 00:38:34,645
‪But what could this be?

887
00:38:39,150 --> 00:38:40,526
‪I think I know what this is.

888
00:38:41,402 --> 00:38:42,236
‪"Can."

889
00:38:42,820 --> 00:38:45,114
‪-What?
‪-"Bird can sing."

890
00:38:45,197 --> 00:38:47,491
‪-"Human can sing."
‪-You're right.

891
00:38:47,575 --> 00:38:50,786
‪-"Fruit cannot sing."
‪-It's a modal verb.

892
00:38:51,996 --> 00:38:54,874
‪-Approaching this in English
‪-Yeah.

893
00:38:54,957 --> 00:38:56,334
‪-actually works better.
‪-Right.

894
00:38:56,917 --> 00:38:59,045
‪That's why I selfishly hoped
‪that you'd come.

895
00:38:59,128 --> 00:39:01,088
‪Thank goodness I did.

896
00:39:04,258 --> 00:39:05,760
‪Is this "face"?

897
00:39:05,843 --> 00:39:07,928
‪-I think it's "head."
‪-Oh, I see.

898
00:39:08,679 --> 00:39:11,098
‪"The human head has eyes."

899
00:39:12,183 --> 00:39:14,060
‪Is this "two"?

900
00:39:14,143 --> 00:39:16,854
‪We already have something else for two.

901
00:39:16,937 --> 00:39:18,856
‪This character is "two."

902
00:39:20,566 --> 00:39:22,068
‪It's weird, isn't it?

903
00:39:22,151 --> 00:39:23,944
‪All of it reads like a riddle.

904
00:39:25,154 --> 00:39:26,322
‪"Eyes."

905
00:39:26,405 --> 00:39:27,907
‪Maybe it's "holes"?

906
00:39:28,699 --> 00:39:29,700
‪Maybe.

907
00:39:29,784 --> 00:39:34,497
‪In other pictures, the people
‪weren't depicted in much detail.

908
00:39:34,580 --> 00:39:39,502
<i>‪But in that picture,</i>
<i>‪the </i><i>‪faces </i><i>‪were drawn with precision.</i>

909
00:39:41,462 --> 00:39:45,508
‪Yeah, look. Ear, eye, eye, ear,
‪nostril, nostril, mouth.

910
00:39:46,175 --> 00:39:47,009
‪Seven?

911
00:39:47,093 --> 00:39:49,136
‪"Holes" could work instead of "eyes."

912
00:39:49,762 --> 00:39:51,597
‪The trident could be "seven."

